) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} - 2 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} - 2 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 x,- 4 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$, $y=- 3 t + 1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2 t,- 4 \\left(- 3 t + 1\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2 t,12 t - 4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t + 1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2 t,12 t - 4⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(12 t - 4\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right) \\\\  & =- 34 t + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =- 34 (1) + 12 \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542528 1080 "60" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542528 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542528 1083 #uuid "5bcd6ddb-a207-5fad-a229-e213b01e5b8b" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542529 1080 "-114" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542529 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542529 1083 #uuid "629864ea-dc15-5e3b-9b4d-74b9fb756902" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542530 1080 "12" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542530 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542530 1083 #uuid "64f6d064-4d09-5ab2-acff-66f1b85596b3" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542531 1080 "2" 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542531 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535923 17592186045443 true] [17592186542531 1083 #uuid "64ffb12f-f951-5c6b-b7c6-f1807ed116e7" 13194139535923 175921860454